1
00:00:01,140 --> 00:00:03,700
Today, we'll look at a line of code together.

2
00:00:04,365 --> 00:00:10,925
Before Swift arrived, developers were already building iPhone apps in a programming language called Objective-C.

3
00:00:11,565 --> 00:00:17,445
To put text or drawings on screen, they used UIKit, Apple’s toolkit for building app interfaces.

4
00:00:18,045 --> 00:00:21,905
The second line sets the starting point for the drawing.

5
00:00:22,525 --> 00:00:27,195
Developers writing apps in Swift could use that same UIKit feature.

6
00:00:27,845 --> 00:00:32,765
We already created a point on the first line, yet the name on the second line says “point” again.

7
00:00:33,365 --> 00:00:40,765
The proposal would change how names were imported to remove repetition and show what each argument was for.

8
00:00:42,150 --> 00:00:44,270
The first line creates a point.

9
00:00:44,905 --> 00:00:49,015
The second line moves the path’s current point there.

10
00:00:49,655 --> 00:00:52,835
We can drop one word from the name on the second line and still understand the call.

11
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:56,130
The word for “point” appears twice.

12
00:00:56,765 --> 00:01:04,935
Doug Gregor and Dave Abrahams proposed removing such repetition when importing Objective-C methods into Swift.

13
00:01:05,595 --> 00:01:10,165
In their December proposal, only the trailing Point disappeared.

14
00:01:10,775 --> 00:01:15,455
To still tells us where to move, and it’s still outside the parentheses.

15
00:01:16,761 --> 00:01:22,740
During the public review the following January, Radek Pietruszewski looked at a method for enumerating a collection.

16
00:01:23,396 --> 00:01:26,726
This method visits the items one by one.

17
00:01:27,376 --> 00:01:30,016
The options specify the order or manner of the visit.

18
00:01:30,596 --> 00:01:37,066
He argued that putting options inside the parentheses makes the next value’s purpose clear.

19
00:01:37,696 --> 00:01:42,676
Doug Gregor then tested a rule that split the name at with.

20
00:01:43,316 --> 00:01:48,646
Applying that rule changed names in 201 Cocoa API files.

21
00:01:50,006 --> 00:01:55,296
In February, the discussion broadened to other words expressing direction or relationships.

22
00:01:55,916 --> 00:02:01,286
Dave Abrahams reported what the guidelines working group found when reading a range of calls.

23
00:02:01,916 --> 00:02:07,266
The group preferred reading the preposition inside the parentheses, together with its argument.

24
00:02:07,906 --> 00:02:11,936
The next day, Doug Gregor implemented the rule in the compiler.

25
00:02:12,551 --> 00:02:18,051
Now the action sits before the parentheses and the destination goes inside.

26
00:02:19,451 --> 00:02:23,341
In some cases, shortening a name could make a difference harder to see.

27
00:02:23,996 --> 00:02:30,056
Assign a Swift array to another variable and append to one: the other’s count stays the same.

28
00:02:30,696 --> 00:02:37,706
If two variables refer to the same mutable array object, both see the added item.

29
00:02:38,366 --> 00:02:46,076
Dave Abrahams warned that removing the NS prefix would make that difference easy to miss.

30
00:02:47,296 --> 00:02:52,976
The March 3 acceptance announcement left Foundation prefix removal to a separate proposal.

31
00:02:54,351 --> 00:02:59,791
Let's return to our opening example in Swift 3, released that September.

32
00:03:00,406 --> 00:03:03,376
The path’s current point is still the same point as before.

33
00:03:03,996 --> 00:03:07,666
Placing the pencil at the starting point leaves the paper unmarked.

34
00:03:08,336 --> 00:03:14,246
Now we’ll connect the corners of a paper airplane, stroking the path after each new segment.

35
00:03:14,896 --> 00:03:19,916
The idea was to make the destination clear without repeating the word for “point”.
